1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,009
Here's something you've felt without noticing.

2
00:00:02,359 --> 00:00:04,541
Your heart and your breathing aren't independent.

3
00:00:04,891 --> 00:00:07,283
Breathe in, and your heart speeds up a little.

4
00:00:07,633 --> 00:00:08,573
But not instantly.

5
00:00:08,923 --> 00:00:10,990
It happens a moment later, with a delay.

6
00:00:12,767 --> 00:00:16,845
Physiologists want to know which systems in the body are linked like this,

7
00:00:16,845 --> 00:00:17,341
and when.

8
00:00:17,691 --> 00:00:20,802
One popular tool for that is called Time Delay Stability.

9
00:00:21,152 --> 00:00:22,639
TDS, for short.

10
00:00:23,633 --> 00:00:26,803
In this video, we'll look at a version that picks its own settings.

11
00:00:27,153 --> 00:00:29,579
We'll call it adaptive TDS.

12
00:00:29,929 --> 00:00:33,958
But to get there, we first need to see how TDS itself thinks.

13
00:00:38,733 --> 00:00:39,151
Okay.

14
00:00:39,501 --> 00:00:41,812
Take a one-minute piece of two signals.

15
00:00:42,162 --> 00:00:44,867
Is the second one just a delayed copy of the first?

16
00:00:45,217 --> 00:00:46,285
Well, let's find out.

17
00:00:46,635 --> 00:00:47,738
We slide it in time.

18
00:00:48,733 --> 00:00:53,110
At every shift, we multiply the two curves point by point, and add it all up.

19
00:00:53,460 --> 00:00:56,608
When the shapes line up, most of those products are positive,

20
00:00:56,608 --> 00:00:57,640
so the sum gets big.

21
00:00:57,990 --> 00:01:00,974
This running score is called the cross-correlation.

22
00:01:01,967 --> 00:01:03,522
And its peak tells us the delay.

23
00:01:03,872 --> 00:01:05,324
We call it τ₀.

24
00:01:05,674 --> 00:01:09,319
Here, the second signal follows the first by 6 seconds.

25
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:15,644
Now, let's do that again.

26
00:01:15,994 --> 00:01:16,575
And again.

27
00:01:16,925 --> 00:01:19,386
Window after window, along the whole recording.

28
00:01:19,736 --> 00:01:21,443
Every window gives us one delay.

29
00:01:21,793 --> 00:01:22,443
One dot.

30
00:01:23,433 --> 00:01:27,508
If two systems really are coupled, the dots line up at the same delay.

31
00:01:27,858 --> 00:01:29,971
If they're not, they just jump around.

32
00:01:30,967 --> 00:01:33,277
TDS turns this into a simple rule.

33
00:01:33,627 --> 00:01:37,676
A window counts as stable when at least four out of five neighbouring delays

34
00:01:37,676 --> 00:01:37,996
agree,

35
00:01:37,996 --> 00:01:39,328
within a small tolerance.

36
00:01:39,678 --> 00:01:43,242
In the published method, that's ±1 second.

37
00:01:44,233 --> 00:01:46,056
Let's fill in the stable windows.

38
00:01:46,406 --> 00:01:50,133
The TDS score is just the share of windows that are stable.

39
00:01:54,900 --> 00:02:00,215
Now, those settings, a 60-second window, a 30-second step, ±1 second,

40
00:02:00,215 --> 00:02:04,606
were chosen for sleep recordings sampled once per second.

41
00:02:04,956 --> 00:02:06,767
And they quietly assume something.

42
00:02:07,117 --> 00:02:10,763
They assume that one-minute holds plenty of independent information.

43
00:02:12,333 --> 00:02:13,587
Look at a fast signal.

44
00:02:13,937 --> 00:02:16,248
Every few seconds, it's doing something new.

45
00:02:16,598 --> 00:02:18,072
Now look at a slow one.

46
00:02:18,422 --> 00:02:21,150
Neighbouring seconds have almost the same value.

47
00:02:21,500 --> 00:02:24,960
So, sixty points, but really only a couple of facts.

48
00:02:26,533 --> 00:02:30,550
And with that little information, the delay in each window is close to random.

49
00:02:30,900 --> 00:02:35,057
Here are two slow signals that really are coupled, with a ten second delay.

50
00:02:35,407 --> 00:02:37,915
Watch what classic TDS does.

51
00:02:38,265 --> 00:02:39,182
The dots scatter.

52
00:02:39,532 --> 00:02:42,899
Its score is under three percent, barely above chance.

53
00:02:47,667 --> 00:02:50,453
So here's the idea behind adaptive TDS.

54
00:02:50,803 --> 00:02:53,009
Before anything else, measure memory.

55
00:02:53,359 --> 00:02:57,445
Put a signal next to a copy of itself, shifted by k seconds, and ask:

56
00:02:57,445 --> 00:02:58,688
how similar are they?

57
00:02:59,667 --> 00:03:01,815
With no shift, they match perfectly.

58
00:03:02,165 --> 00:03:03,593
A correlation of one.

59
00:03:03,943 --> 00:03:06,021
As the shift grows, the match fades.

60
00:03:06,371 --> 00:03:08,275
That curve is the autocorrelation.

61
00:03:08,625 --> 00:03:11,806
And notice, a fast signal forgets within seconds.

62
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:15,965
The Bartlett factor adds up the squared autocorrelation,

63
00:03:15,965 --> 00:03:17,943
until the curve first crosses zero.

64
00:03:18,293 --> 00:03:21,474
B equals one, plus twice the sum of r squared.

65
00:03:21,824 --> 00:03:26,178
Think of it as: how many samples in a row are worth one independent sample?

66
00:03:27,167 --> 00:03:29,268
For the fast signal, B is about 2.

67
00:03:29,618 --> 00:03:31,847
For the slow one, it's about 31.

68
00:03:32,197 --> 00:03:36,774
So 31 seconds of that slow signal carry roughly one second's worth of new

69
00:03:36,774 --> 00:03:37,526
information.

70
00:03:42,300 --> 00:03:43,148
Step two.

71
00:03:43,498 --> 00:03:46,969
Make the window long enough to hold about thirty independent samples.

72
00:03:47,319 --> 00:03:49,594
So the window is thirty times B.

73
00:03:49,944 --> 00:03:52,661
And we take B from the slowest signal in the recording,

74
00:03:52,661 --> 00:03:55,181
so that every pair is measured with the same ruler.

75
00:03:56,167 --> 00:03:59,220
For our slow pair, that's a window of about 15 minutes.

76
00:03:59,570 --> 00:04:00,046
Not one.

77
00:04:01,033 --> 00:04:03,704
Of course, a longer window means coarser timing.

78
00:04:04,054 --> 00:04:07,339
So you set a cap: the longest window you're willing to accept.

79
00:04:07,689 --> 00:04:10,058
For the sleep data, we used five minutes.

80
00:04:14,833 --> 00:04:15,634
Step three.

81
00:04:15,984 --> 00:04:18,188
The window moves forward by half its length,

82
00:04:18,188 --> 00:04:21,093
and the delay search grows with it, up to half the window.

83
00:04:21,443 --> 00:04:23,464
Slow systems can have long delays,

84
00:04:23,464 --> 00:04:26,911
and a fixed 30-second search would simply never find them.

85
00:04:28,500 --> 00:04:30,648
There's one exception, and that's rhythms.

86
00:04:30,998 --> 00:04:33,259
If a signal repeats every T seconds,

87
00:04:33,259 --> 00:04:37,970
then a delay of just over half a period looks exactly like a negative delay

88
00:04:37,970 --> 00:04:39,729
of just under half a period.

89
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:40,979
See?

90
00:04:41,329 --> 00:04:43,105
The two overlays look the same.

91
00:04:43,455 --> 00:04:46,752
So the search stops at half of the shortest rhythm in the data.

92
00:04:51,533 --> 00:04:53,310
Step four is the subtle one.

93
00:04:53,660 --> 00:04:54,472
The tolerance.

94
00:04:54,822 --> 00:04:58,211
With a long window, ±1 second is way too strict,

95
00:04:58,211 --> 00:05:00,894
because a real delay wanders a little.

96
00:05:01,244 --> 00:05:03,147
But if you make the tolerance too loose,

97
00:05:03,147 --> 00:05:05,192
chance alone starts to look like stability.

98
00:05:06,767 --> 00:05:09,553
So, adaptive TDS asks the data.

99
00:05:09,903 --> 00:05:13,595
It takes one signal and rotates it in time by a large random amount.

100
00:05:13,945 --> 00:05:16,569
Whatever falls off the end wraps around to the start.

101
00:05:16,919 --> 00:05:19,345
Each signal keeps its own shape and rhythm.

102
00:05:19,695 --> 00:05:21,948
But any real coupling between them is gone.

103
00:05:23,533 --> 00:05:28,119
Then it runs TDS on these fake pairs, while slowly widening the tolerance.

104
00:05:28,469 --> 00:05:32,215
And it keeps the widest tolerance at which the fake pairs still look coupled

105
00:05:32,215 --> 00:05:34,089
no more than five percent of the time.

106
00:05:35,067 --> 00:05:38,596
For our slow pair, that's ±43 seconds.

107
00:05:38,946 --> 00:05:39,503
That's wide.

108
00:05:39,853 --> 00:05:40,538
But it's earned.

109
00:05:40,888 --> 00:05:43,686
With it, chance stays at or below five percent.

110
00:05:48,467 --> 00:05:51,369
Now let's put it all together, on that same slow pair.

111
00:05:51,719 --> 00:05:54,529
Classic TDS, with one-minute windows.

112
00:05:54,879 --> 00:05:55,796
The dots scatter.

113
00:05:56,146 --> 00:05:59,432
Adaptive TDS, with 15 minute windows.

114
00:05:59,782 --> 00:06:02,858
And now, the dots lock onto the true ten second delay.

115
00:06:03,208 --> 00:06:07,109
Half of its windows are stable, against a chance level of zero.

116
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:10,047
That was just one example.

117
00:06:10,397 --> 00:06:14,002
But in earlier tests, with pairs whose coupling and delay were known,

118
00:06:14,002 --> 00:06:14,890
the pattern held.

119
00:06:15,240 --> 00:06:19,187
As the signals get slower, classic TDS falls to chance.

120
00:06:19,537 --> 00:06:22,951
Adaptive TDS stays close to the best possible setting.

121
00:06:23,301 --> 00:06:25,634
That's the one you'd pick if you already knew the answer.

122
00:06:30,433 --> 00:06:31,734
What about real data?

123
00:06:32,084 --> 00:06:35,963
On sleep recordings from Bashan's study, thirty five people,

124
00:06:35,963 --> 00:06:38,225
both methods find the known result.

125
00:06:38,575 --> 00:06:41,257
The body's network is weakest in deep sleep.

126
00:06:41,607 --> 00:06:44,301
Here, adaptive TDS doesn't add much.

127
00:06:44,651 --> 00:06:45,771
And that makes sense,

128
00:06:45,771 --> 00:06:49,237
because the classic settings were designed for exactly this data.

129
00:06:50,833 --> 00:06:52,258
And on one of your runs,

130
00:06:52,258 --> 00:06:56,592
the fixed app setting found no stable delay between speed and heart rate.

131
00:06:56,942 --> 00:06:59,464
Adaptive TDS found 5 stable windows,

132
00:06:59,464 --> 00:07:03,107
with heart rate following speed by about 16 seconds.

133
00:07:07,900 --> 00:07:09,572
Of course, nothing is free.

134
00:07:09,922 --> 00:07:11,988
A long window blurs short events.

135
00:07:12,338 --> 00:07:16,112
A five-minute window can't isolate a two-minute stretch of being awake.

136
00:07:16,462 --> 00:07:18,935
And a wide tolerance blurs the exact delay.

137
00:07:20,533 --> 00:07:21,845
So, let's recap.

138
00:07:22,195 --> 00:07:24,842
Adaptive TDS, in four steps.

139
00:07:25,192 --> 00:07:27,154
One: measure each signal's memory.

140
00:07:27,504 --> 00:07:31,138
Two: size the window to hold enough independent samples.

141
00:07:31,488 --> 00:07:33,590
Three: let the delay search grow with it.

142
00:07:33,940 --> 00:07:37,806
Four: calibrate the tolerance on pairs where the coupling has been destroyed.

143
00:07:38,156 --> 00:07:41,604
And then, choose the cap that matches the time resolution you need.

144
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:44,794
And that's adaptive Time Delay Stability.

145
00:07:45,144 --> 00:07:46,050
Thanks for watching.

